      "role": "reasoning": "Learner successfully completes the problem. Finalize with full answer format, formula breakdown, and exam tip.",
      "content": "$F_R = \\sqrt{110{,}03^2 + 332{,}60^2} = 350{,}33\\text{ N}$ at $71{,}69^\\circ$ North of East! 🎉\n\n**Final Answer:** $350{,}33\\text{ N}$ at $71{,}69^\\circ$ North of East (or $\\text{N } 18{,}31^\\circ\\text{ E}$)\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $F_R = \\sqrt{F_x^2 + F_y^2}$ and $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{F_y}{F_x}\\right)$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $F_x$ is the sum of all horizontal force components (left/right forces combined)\n  * $F_y$ is the sum of all vertical force components (up/down forces combined)\n  * $F_R$ is the final combined resultant force magnitude\n  * $\\theta$ is the direction angle relative to the reference axis\n* **Why we use it:** When multiple forces pull in different oblique directions, we cannot simply add their magnitudes. By splitting them into perpendicular $x$ and $y$ grid directions, we can easily sum them into a flat right-angled triangle and use Pythagoras theorem!\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always remember to include both **magnitude** and **direction** (e.g., compass bearing or angle relative to an axis) for vector answers. Omitting direction loses you the final accuracy mark in DBE exams!"
